<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Derbent</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bab al-Abwab, Darband</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Derbent is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, on a narrow strip of land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> mountains. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> fortified the pass in the 6th century with long stone walls running from the sea into the hills, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> called it Bab al-Abwab, the Gate of Gates. Its citadel and walls are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, and it is often called the oldest city in Russia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/derbent.jpg" alt="Derbent" width="320" height="204" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Шамиль Магомедов · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Derbent_city._Dagestan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The greater danger came from the north: the Alans, and later the Huns and other steppe peoples, crossed the Caucasus by the mountain passes and by the narrow Caspian coastal route at <b>Derbent</b>, and in 135 an Alan invasion struck Albania and Media.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caucasian-albania">Caucasian Albania · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The dynasty ruled from Shamakhi, also called Yazidiyya after the family, and gradually extended its authority over neighbouring districts, absorbing Layzan in the early tenth century and at times controlling the city of <b>Derbent</b> to the north.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvanshah State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1772 their chiefs established a ruling house based at Amb and later at <b>Darband</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/amb-state">Amb State</a></li><li><blockquote>After Nader Shah's death in 1747, the Persian governor of <b>Derbent</b>, Muhammad Husayn Khan, ruled the walled city and its hinterland as an independent khan.</blockquote><a href="/state/derbent-khanate">Derbent Khanate</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Derbent</b>, called Bab al-Abwab, the Gate of Gates, by the Arabs, had been fortified by the Sasanians with walls running from the Caspian into the mountains.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-derbent">Emirate of Derbent</a></li><li><blockquote>Kaitag, in the foothills and mountains west of <b>Derbent</b>, was ruled by a dynasty bearing the title utsmi from at least the 15th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/kaitag-utsmiyate">Kaitag Utsmiyate</a></li></ul></section>
